[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 703,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 466,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105867,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 466,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Congenital haemangiomas are benign vascular tumours that are fully grown at birth, with the exception of a subgroup having undergone involution during fetal life.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:39:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 704,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 467,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105868,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 467,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9131/0 Congenital haemangioma</p>\r\n<p>9131/0 Rapidly involuting congenital haemangioma with fetal involution</p>\r\n<p>9131/0 Rapidly involuting congenital haemangioma</p>\r\n<p>9131/0 Partially involuting congenital haemangioma</p>\r\n<p>9131/0 Non-involuting congenital haemangioma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:39:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 705,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 468,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105869,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 468,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2E81.2Y &amp; XH27G6 Other specified benign vascular lesion of infancy and childhood &amp; Congenital haemangioma, NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:39:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 706,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 469,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105870,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 469,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> congenital haemangioma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> capillary haemangioma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:39:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 707,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 470,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105871,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Rapidly involuting congenital haemangioma with fetal involution; rapidly involuting congenital haemangioma (RICH); partially involuting congenital haemangioma (PICH); non-involuting congenital haemangioma (NICH)</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:39:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105872,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>All congenital haemangiomas have a predilection for the head and neck region and the lower extremities.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:39:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105873,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>RICH with fetal involution is virtually regressed by birth, leaving only a cutaneous stain {25492638}. RICH, PICH, and NICH are well-circumscribed lesions usually measuring several centimetres in diameter. They are raised and pink, blue-red, or purple, with superficial telangiectasia and sometimes a pale halo. Some RICHs and PICHs also have central depression, ulceration, or scarring. Neonatal thrombocytopenia develops with some RICHs and PICHs. Arteriovenous shunting occurs rarely in RICH {11735711; 11391180; 15018449; 32281140}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:39:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105874,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Congenital haemangiomas are rare. There is no sex predominance.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:39:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105875,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:39:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105876,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>GNAQ</em> and <em>GNA11</em> somatic missense variants identified in RICH and NICH have been shown to constitutively activate MAPK and/or YAP1 signalling {27259057}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:39:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105877,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>RICH and NICH have a red homogeneous surface on sectioning. Occasional RICHs have foci of fibrosis or cysts.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:39:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105878,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>All congenital haemangiomas involve skin and hypodermis. In RICH with fetal involution, a few small clusters of capillaries, enlarged dysmorphic veins, arteries with intimal fibrosis, and dilated lymphatic channels are present within a fibrotic dermis and hypodermis. At an early age, RICH, PICH, and NICH are usually similar, with lobules of capillaries with round or stellate lumina, and bland endothelial cells and pericytes. In RICH, regression is accompanied by smaller lobules; apparent loss of lobules; dysmorphic veins; extensive fibrous stroma; and occasionally haemosiderin, calcifications, and cystic spaces. Some RICHs display these changes at birth, indicating a degree of prenatal involution. NICHs in older children tend to have variably sized and often large lobules and prominent interlobular arteries and dysmorphic veins that at times incorporate lobules. The endothelium is hobnailed and pink; PAS-positive, diastase-resistant cytoplasmic globules often appear. All congenital haemangiomas have GLUT1-immunonegative endothelium {11735711; 11391180; 15018449; 32281140}. Transient and focal intralobular endothelial immunopositivity for D2-40 has been reported in RICH and PICH in association with neonatal thrombocytopenia {32281140; 25492638}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:39:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105879,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:39:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 478,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105880,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 478,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most RICHs and NICHs have <em>GNAQ</em> or <em>GNA11</em> mutations {27259057}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:39:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 718,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 481,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105881,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 481,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a lobular capillary lesion in RICH, PICH, and NICH, supported by clinical and imaging features.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> detection of <em>GNAQ</em> or <em>GNA11</em> mutations.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:39:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 716,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 479,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105882,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 479,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:39:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 717,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 480,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105883,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 162,
        "headingId": 480,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most RICHs involute in &lt;&nbsp;1&nbsp;year. PICHs partially involute and then stabilize. NICHs do not involute: their growth is proportional to that of the child, and a few show extension and increased blood flow over time {11735711; 11391180; 15018449; 32281140}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:39:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]